#: ../../source/docs/reverse-thrust-autolanding.rst:5
#: feb735663fa24c20934905dda41cd6a2
msgid "Reverse Thrust Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/reverse-thrust-autolanding.rst:7
#: de2cad8655e64980b245039a3a05c97c
msgid "Some ESC's allow for reversing motor direction. When using reverse on the propeller it will generate a negative thrust which can be used to reduce your airspeed. During a steep landing approach this method can be used to maintain a stable and low airspeed allowing you to land much more softly and precisely. Reverse thrust can also be automatically used during automatic landings (see below) or during other throttle controlled modes to steepen descents. See  :ref:`Reverse Thrust Setup<reverse-thrust-setup>` for details on setting up reverse thrust."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/reverse-thrust-autolanding.rst:10
#: 35f347918d86465c958fbf1b4d65d533
msgid "enabling reverse thrust automatically sets the SERVOx_TRIM value to LOW throttle stick for the throttle output servo. SERVOx_TRIM is normally ignored, and low stick is SERVOx_MIN! Arming in this condition with an ESC not properly setup will spin-up the motor, possibly dramatically!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/reverse-thrust-autolanding.rst:13
#: 992d2663c7cd42eea5eb452ed1e19256
msgid "Reverse-Thrust in AutoLanding and Throttle Controlled Modes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/reverse-thrust-autolanding.rst:15
#: 43b9b0589f8846cc830573298a258ed6
msgid "To use this feature in automatic landings, it is highly recommend to use an airspeed sensor and a rangefinder (see above) for an accurate altitude."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/reverse-thrust-autolanding.rst:17
#: e6dfc2c40f3241adb021811116bf4b7d
msgid "The below video is an example of a Skywalker X8 performing an auto-landing with a 15 degree slope. The target is the hat on the ground showing it is possible to get repeatable high precision landings where the final position error was dictated by the GPS position error. This particular aircraft has been landed at 20deg and 25deg slopes too. You may have different results depending on weight of aircraft and available thrust from motor/propeller. Typically a Skywalker X8 would need a shallow slope such at 6 to 10deg."
msgstr ""

